1
00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:03.870
Welcome to Safe and Reliable AI
via Guardrails

2
00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:05.772
built in partnership with GuardrailsAI.

3
00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:10.477
Guardrails are safety mechanisms
and validation tools built

4
00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:14.447
into AI applications, especially those
that use large language models or

5
00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:18.318
LLMs to ensure at runtime
that the application follows

6
00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:21.988
specific rules and operates
within predefined boundaries.

7
00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:27.227
Guardrails act as a protective framework,
preventing unintended outputs

8
00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:31.331
from LLMs and aligning the behavior
with the developer's expectations.

9
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:35.902
They also provide
a critical layer of control and oversight

10
00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:40.440
within your application, and supports
building safe and responsible AI.

11
00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:45.178
This course will show you how to build
robust guardrails from scratch

12
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:50.350
that mitigate common failure modes of
LLM part applications, like hallucinations

13
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.587
or inadvertently revealing
personally identifiable information.

14
00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.657
I'm delighted to introduce Shreya Rajpal,

15
00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:02.395
who is CEO and Co-Founder of GuardrailsAI
and your instructor for this course.

16
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:06.433
Shreya has
been working on AI reliability problems

17
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.669
for most of her career,
including for self-driving car systems,

18
00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:14.507
where reliable behavior is critical
for the safety of pedestrians and riders.

19
00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.510
And that's
when she and I first met many years ago.

20
00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.046
She is also a founding engineer
at Predibase,

21
00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:23.416
and so she's deeply familiar
with building LLM systems.

22
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.385
Welcome, Shreya.

23
00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:27.687
Thanks, Andrew. It's great to be here.

24
00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.257
I'm really excited to show you
how Guardrails can help you create

25
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:34.494
reliable chatbot applications, and help
you realize

26
00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.630
the full potential of LLMs
to power your projects.

27
00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:38.898
I've talked to many

28
00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:42.469
teams that are working to build
innovative applications using LLMs.

29
00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:46.573
The availability of APIs access
powerful models like GPT-4,

30
00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:50.910
Claude, Gemini, and many others
has allowed developers to quickly

31
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.347
build prototypes, which is great
for early stage development.

32
00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:59.919
But when you want to move beyond to proof
of concept, teams often encounter problems

33
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:03.756
with a reliable LLM that lies
at the heart of your application.

34
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:08.928
The core challenge is that the output
of LLMs is hard to predict in advance.

35
00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.731
While the techniques have
helped significantly

36
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.000
things
like prompting techniques or model fine

37
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.937
tuning, or alignment
methods like RLHF or RAG techniques.

38
00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:19.973
All of these techniques have helped.

39
00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:23.409
But the core problem is
they still can't fully

40
00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:26.579
eliminate
output variability and unpredictability.

41
00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.450
So this can lead to significant
challenges, especially when

42
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.287
designing applications for industry
with strict registry requirements,

43
00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:37.924
or for clients
that demand high levels of consistency.

44
00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:42.729
Developers often find that techniques
like RLHF and RAG are insufficient

45
00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:46.633
on their own to meet the stringent
reliability and compliance standards

46
00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:49.836
that are required for many real-world
applications.

47
00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.772
At GuardrailsAI,
we work with many clients

48
00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.775
in sectors
like healthcare, government, and finance.

49
00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:59.646
Who are really excited about the
possibilities of LLMs for their businesses,

50
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:03.116
but they can't use them in their products
because on their own

51
00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.652
LLMs aren't trustworthy enough.

52
00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:07.687
This is where guardrails come in.

53
00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:11.157
These additional components
of AI applications work

54
00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.525
to check that the output

55
00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:16.462
or input of an LLM conforms
to a set of rules or guidelines,

56
00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.666
and this can be used to prevent
incorrect, irrelevant

57
00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:22.669
or sensitive information
from being revealed to users.

58
00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:26.739
Implementing guardrails
in your applications can really help you

59
00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.542
move out
beyond the proof of concept phase,

60
00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.911
and get your application
ready for production.

61
00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.947
At the heart of the guardrails
implementation,

62
00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:37.350
you learn in this course is a component
called the validator.

63
00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:42.455
This is a function that takes as input
a user prompt and or the response

64
00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:47.327
from an LLM, and checks to make sure
that it conforms to a predefined rule.

65
00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.363
Validators can be quite simple.

66
00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:51.130
For example,

67
00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.500
if you want to build a validator,
that checks if the text contains

68
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.837
any personally identifiable information
or PII.

69
00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:01.140
You can maybe use
a simple regex expression to check

70
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.877
for phone numbers
or emails or similar types of PII data,

71
00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:09.849
and if any are present, then
have the application throw the exception

72
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.019
to prevent the information
from being reveled to the user.

73
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.021
You can also create more

74
00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:18.891
advanced validators that use machine
learning models, like Transformers

75
00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:23.796
or even other LLMs, to carry out
more complex analysis of the text.

76
00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.067
This can help you build systems that,
for example, can help with chatbots

77
00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:32.038
stay on topic by checking against a list
of allowed discussion subjects,

78
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:36.109
or prevent specific words
from being included in an LLMs response,

79
00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.647
which is useful for avoiding trademark
terms or mentions of competitive names.

80
00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:45.385
You can even use guardrails
to help reduce hallucinations from LLMs.

81
00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:50.390
In one of the lessons in this course,
you'll use a natural language inference

82
00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:55.028
or NLI model to build a hallucination
validator that checks

83
00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:58.264
whether the answer to a question in a RAG
system

84
00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:01.868
is actually grounded
in the words of the retrieved text.

85
00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.371
This means that the source text
actually confirms

86
00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:08.374
the truthfulness of whatever the LLMs just
generated.

87
00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:12.712
Guardrails are very flexible,
and you can make use of many smaller

88
00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:15.948
machine learning models to carry out
validation tasks.

89
00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:19.485
This helps
keep your application performant

90
00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:23.990
and actually results in higher reliability
than using LLMs alone

91
00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:27.093
for some failure modes.
This course will walk you

92
00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:31.164
through the coding pattern that we use
to build guardrails at our company,

93
00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:35.168
and you'll implement individual guardrails
for a number of failure modes.

94
00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:39.138
You'll also learn how to access
many pre-built guardrails

95
00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:42.141
that are available on the Guardrails hub.

96
00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.878
After completing the course,
you'll be able to modify this pattern

97
00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:47.714
and build your own guardrails

98
00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:50.983
that are customized
for your specific product or use case.

99
00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:54.921
I'd like to thank Zayd Simjee
from GuardrailsAI and Tommy

100
00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:56.122
Nelson from DeepLearning.AI,

101
00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:59.025
who have worked to create this course.

102
00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:03.162
A lot of companies worry about safety
and reliability of LLM based systems,

103
00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:07.100
and this is slowing down investments
in building such systems.

104
00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:10.603
Fortunately,
putting guardrails on your system

105
00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:14.974
makes a huge difference in terms
of creating safe, reliable applications.

106
00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:20.046
So, I think the tools in this course
will unlock for you more opportunities

107
00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:24.751
to build and deploy
LLM-powered applications. Specifically,

108
00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:28.221
the next time someone expresses
a worry to you about safety,

109
00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.624
reliability,
or hallucinations of LLM-based systems,

110
00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:36.129
I think this course will help you answer
in a way that helps reassure them.

111
00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.332
So that, let's go on to the next video

112
00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:42.435
where you explore
the basic failure modes of a chatbot.

